  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 340 PM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nAn upper-level ridge is currently over our area, which has resulted \nin drier conditions and slightly above average temperatures over the \nlast couple of days. This ridging pattern is going to come to a \nclose as the feature progresses to our east as we shift to seeing \nthe influence of a cut-off low currently over the desert southwest. \nThe low will continue to progress eastward, and interact with the \nremnants of Hurricane Polo across northern Mexico. As a result, flow \nwill be enhanced, and additional moisture will be advected into our \nregion. The big story will be the high PWAT values potentially \nexceeding 2\", and combining it with our trough will contribute to a \nflooding concern across northwest Missouri. We have given a flood \nwatch over those areas as the high PWAT values, moisture content, \nand system duration will lead to heavy rainfall and training. These \nfactors will result in areas seeing 2\"-4\" of rainfall, with locally \nhigher amounts possible over the next few days.\n\nSoutherly flow associated with the ridge has allowed for \ntemperatures to get up into the 80s, along with moisture transport \nfrom the Gulf. Our upper-level ridge progression today will allow a \nlow-level jet to progress over the area, which is resulting in \nisolated showers throughout this afternoon and evening. The low-\nlevel jet will strengthen tonight, and rainfall chances will become \nmuch more widespread tonight, and last through late tomorrow \nmorning, especially in Kansas and areas north of Interstate 70 in \nMissouri. A few rumbles of thunder may be possible with this round \nof activity, but the highest chances of seeing actual thunderstorms \nwill be in Kansas, due to higher amounts of instability staying out \nthere. Today and tonight's rainfall will have an effect on how high \ntemperatures get for Wednesday. Areas north of Interstate 70, which \nwill see the greatest coverage of showers and cloud cover will \nremain in the 70s, while our southern counties that stay dry may end \nup pushing into the lower 90s.\n\nAfter a quick break Wednesday afternoon and evening, the remnants of \nstorms that develop across central Kansas and Oklahoma will make \ntheir way to the area Wednesday night into Thursday morning. Chances \nto see sub-severe storms will possible with this round of activity, \nas the upper-low pressure system progresses eastward and gives us \ncold-air aloft to get higher values of instability. On-and-off storm \nchances are anticipated to persist throughout the day Thursday, and \nwill continue to contribute to the flooding risk. A cold front will \nthen pass through Thursday night, and therefore, will help push off \nthe rain and high temperatures. Our trough and closed-low will \ncontinue to progress through the area, and will merge in with zonal \nflow across the southern Canadian border. Zonal flow to our north \nwill lead to a break from the rain starting Friday morning and \nthroughout the weekend. Cannot rule out an isolated rain shower, but \nhigh pressure will advect cooler air from the north, resulting in a \ndrier and more seasonable weekend.",
